Accurate fault diagnosis of power transformers is critical for power system reliability. While acoustic-based diagnosis offers non-contact measurement and low sensor cost, existing methods struggle with single-modality representations, background noise, and limited labeled data. This paper proposes a multi-modal framework integrating Mel spectrograms with Gramian Angular Difference Fields (GADF) into three-channel fused representations, enhanced by a Pulse-Coupled Neural Network (PCNN) for noise suppression. A physics-informed Laplacian regularization, derived from the acoustic Helmholtz equation, constrains learned features toward physically smooth solutions, enabling the model to distinguish fault-induced irregularities from unstructured noise. Evaluated on a real-world transformer dataset of five conditions with session-aware data partitioning, the method undergoes extensive validation including component-wise ablation, five-fold cross-validation, hyperparameter sensitivity analysis, and noise robustness tests. The proposed framework achieves state-of-the-art accuracy while maintaining robust performance under low signal-to-noise ratio conditions, outperforming seven representative methods including Vision Transformer, EfficientNet, Swin Transformer, and end-to-end 1D-CNN.
